# =====================================================================
# DELIBERATE FIXTURE — NOT A PRODUCT DEFECT.
# Lane: unstable_test_mode (lane C, test/fixtures/unstable_mode/**).
# This spec FAILS AN ASSERTION ON PURPOSE so the runner's ERROR (real
# failure) outcome class has something honest to detect. Do not "fix" it.
#
# @skip  <- keeps it out of DEFAULT discovery (test_runner_files.spl skips
#           any file whose content contains "# @skip").
# Selecting it back: `--only-skipped` does NOT match "# @skip" -- it matches the
# literal selector `tag: "skip"` (test_runner_files.spl, slow path; the manifest
# fast path uses entry.skip_tag). Both markers are therefore required:
# "# @skip" to stay out, `tag: "skip"` to be reachable by explicit request.
# Without the second one the directory run discovers 0 files and reports
# "No test files found" with exit 4 -- measured.
# =====================================================================
"""
## Purpose and audience
A deliberate failing-assertion fixture for the unstable-test-mode lane:
the scenario asserts 1 == 2 so the runner's ERROR (real failure) outcome
class has something honest to detect. For runner operators and CI owners
verifying that a genuine assertion failure is classified as ERROR and not
as a crash, a skip, or a pass.
## Operator workflow
Select it back explicitly (it is skip-tagged out of default discovery) and
expect a non-zero exit with exactly 1 failed example:
./src/compiler_rust/target/bootstrap/simple run test/fixtures/unstable_mode/fail_spec.spl --only-skipped
## Compatibility and limitations
The assertion is intentionally a constant-false comparison; the scorer's
real-assertion rules therefore stay unsatisfied on purpose, and this file
is expected to keep a lower documentization score than a normal spec.
Do not "repair" the comparison — a passing fixture deletes the only proof
the ERROR outcome class works.
## Verification guidance and troubleshooting
A healthy run reports outcome=ERROR, executed=1, failed=1. A pass here
means the fixture was weakened — restore the failing assertion and file a
bug against whatever change made it pass.
"""
# Lifecycle: doc/01_research/infra/sspec/modern_sspec_typed_evidence_research_2026-08-08.md ;
# doc/03_plan/infra/testing/sspec_prevention_mock_plan_2026-08-07.md ;
# doc/04_architecture/test/00_test_architecture.md ;
# doc/05_design/infra/test_runner/unstable_mode_scope_finding_2026-08-17.md
use std.spec.step

use std.spec.*

# @manual_section: unstable mode failing-assertion fixture
describe "unstable mode failing-assertion fixture":
    # @manual scenario evidence
    it "fails a genuine assertion on purpose":
        # @req REQ-TEST-RUNNER-ERROR-001
        # @capture(statistics): failed-example count for the ERROR outcome class
        step("assert an intentionally false comparison so the runner records a failure")
        expect(1).to_equal(2)  # oracle: deliberately false — this fixture exists to fail
